WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:10.180
A sympathetic neuron collects a survival factor called NGF at the tip of its axon.

2
00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:13.800
The target releases it near the axon terminals.

3
00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.820
That tip can sit centimetres from the cell body.

4
00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:23.500
The tip receives the signal, but the nucleus decides whether the cell lives.

5
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.440
So how does the message get home?

6
00:00:27.283 --> 00:00:29.343
Could the signal simply drift?

7
00:00:30.323 --> 00:00:34.683
Diffusion is slow, because time grows with the square of the distance.

8
00:00:35.543 --> 00:00:40.143
For a typical soluble protein, one centimetre takes roughly two months.

9
00:00:40.923 --> 00:00:44.803
A phosphorylated kinase would be switched off long before it arrived.

10
00:00:45.343 --> 00:00:47.283
So the signal needs a vehicle.

11
00:00:47.823 --> 00:00:53.217
It should stay active for hours and carry the identity of the factor that started it.

13
00:00:53.217 --> 00:01:01.177
At the tip, NGF binds its receptor, TrkA. Ligand and receptor are pulled into the

14
00:01:01.177 --> 00:01:05.517
cell together, in a small membrane bubble called the signaling endosome.

15
00:01:06.517 --> 00:01:11.497
The motor protein dynein drags it along microtubules toward the cell body.

16
00:01:12.437 --> 00:01:17.317
At roughly one micrometre each second, one centimetre takes a few hours.

17
00:01:17.317 --> 00:01:21.437
This carried model is favoured, but not proven.

18
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:29.320
Now a test. In the mouse, sympathetic axons grow out along blood vessels, which make

19
00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:33.200
NT-3. The final targets make NGF.

20
00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:39.180
Both factors can switch on TrkA. Which one keeps the neuron alive?

21
00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:46.310
Give each factor only to the distal axons of mouse sympathetic neurons in culture.

22
00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:53.570
NT-3 supports local growth through TrkA, but not survival of the cell bodies.

23
00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:56.190
NGF supports both.

24
00:01:57.010 --> 00:01:58.630
The difference is trafficking.

25
00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:07.790
Complexes of NGF and TrkA are carried back, but complexes of NT-3 and TrkA are not.

27
00:02:07.883 --> 00:02:13.843
So the factor that decides survival must be one whose signal can reach the nucleus.

28
00:02:14.503 --> 00:02:17.963
A factor that acts only locally can steer an axon.

29
00:02:18.303 --> 00:02:20.183
It cannot select a neuron.

30
00:02:21.583 --> 00:02:25.483
Competition between neurons for the final target therefore depends on the

31
00:02:25.483 --> 00:02:28.303
trafficking of the receptor, not only on binding.

32
00:02:29.283 --> 00:02:34.563
Keep this rule. The factor that decides survival is the one whose signal can travel.
